<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/advanced/neuropsychology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Neuropsychology studies <strong>brain–behaviour relationships through damage and
    disease</strong> — the clinical science of what each brain region contributes, inferred from
    what breaks when it breaks. It remains the most causal evidence base in human cognitive
    neuroscience (you can't lesion volunteers), and its assessments guide rehabilitation after
    stroke, injury and dementia.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the lesion logic, the famous syndromes, and recovery science" widget="equationStep" config={neuroCfg} />

  <h2>Assessment in practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Batteries map the profile:</strong> memory (WMS), intelligence (WAIS), executive function (Trail Making, Wisconsin Card Sort), language (Boston). The pattern — not any single score — localises and diagnoses (dementia type, TBI extent).</li>
    <li><strong>Executive function and the frontal lobes:</strong> planning, inhibition, flexibility — the "simulator" tests. Frontal damage spares IQ while destroying judgement: intelligence and wisdom are dissociable.</li>
    <li><strong>Dementia assessment:</strong> distinguishing Alzheimer's (memory-first), vascular (stepwise), frontotemporal (behaviour-first) — pattern + imaging + biomarkers. Early diagnosis is now possible years before symptoms (the medicine ladder connects).</li>
    <li><strong>Everyday neuropsychology:</strong> concussion protocols in sport, return-to-work decisions after injury, capacity assessments (can this patient consent?). The subfield most people actually meet.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Lezak, Neuropsychological Assessment; Shallice, From Neuropsychology to Cognitive Structure; Mesulam, Principles of Behavioral Neurology.</p>
</TopicPage>
